00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:05.490
CAMERON BALAHAN: Hi.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.410
I'm Cameron, and today
I'm going to talk

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.720
about the latest release of
the Go programming language

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.890
Go 1.18.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.290
This is a huge release
that includes new features

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.410
like fuzzing, workspace mode,
performance improvements,

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.250
and our biggest change ever
to the language, generics.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.800
Supporting generics has been
our most-requested feature

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:23.790
since Go 1.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.760
In Go 1.18, we've delivered
the generic support

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.490
for the majority of
our users need today.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.720
And there's more coming.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.720
In future releases, expect
us to introduce more generics

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.805
in the Go standard library and
continue to iterate and provide

00:00:36.805 --> 00:00:38.430
additional support
for some of the more

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.950
complicated generic use cases.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.080
We want your feedback,
which we'll use

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.770
to learn what we can do better.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.190
In fact, that's our approach
to this whole release.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.600
We huge changes are
hard to get right.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.650
So in Go 1.18, we've laid
the foundation for features

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.760
that we anticipate we can make
better in the releases to come.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.000
Before generics, writing
generic code could be tedious.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.130
It often involved
having multiple methods

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.620
that did exactly the same
thing but with different types

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.430
of names.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.740
Many in the community
heavily relied on Go

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.970
generate to make
this a little easier.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.850
But even then, you
were usually left

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.440
with huge blocks
of type switches

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.140
for calling your
generic functions.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.510
Other times, libraries
made design decisions

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.750
about using a type that was a
least common denominator type.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.180
For example, take a
look at the map package.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.340
Common methods like
Min and Max were forced

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.200
to operate on a float 64.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.020
But what if you wanted to
figure out the Max of two ints?

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.690
Pre 1.18, you would
have to cast your types

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.600
to floats to call the functions
and cast the result back

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.000
to an int after
you get the result.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.100
The solution to the
Go generics problem

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.880
needed to solve two issues.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.660
One, it needed a way to
provide parameterized types

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.640
for strucks functions
and methods.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.100
Two, it needed a way
for parameterized types

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.650
to use common operators,
like equals, less than,

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.270
and greater than.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.910
I would also like to point
out that this example makes

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.950
use of the currently
experimental constraints

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.140
package that is being
considered for adoption

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.790
into the standard library.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.860
As you would expect,
generics gives us

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.070
a way to create parameterized
types and function signatures.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.919
Rather than write out multiple
functions for the same behavior

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:13.320
on different types, you
can write one function

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.940
and use it across many types.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.040
But this does introduce
some new complexity,

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.740
which we strive to avoid in Go.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.600
Because of this,
the implementation

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.430
tries to take advantage
of some concepts

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.190
that most Gophers are
already familiar with.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.820
The two most important concepts
are constraints and type sets.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.590
Constraints are just interfaces.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.860
They can also include a new
concept called type sets.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.380
And type sets are the
union of types allowed

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.670
to be used by the constraint.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.150
Next, my colleague Cody
will take a closer look

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.190
at how generics work in Go.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.330
CODY OSS: Thanks, Cameron.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.610
As Cameron mentioned,
I am Cody, and I

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.610
would like to walk you through
how generics, also referred

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.695
to as parameterized
types, work and how

00:02:53.695 --> 00:02:55.320
they are different
from implementations

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.966
in other languages that
you may be familiar with.

00:02:58.966 --> 00:03:01.790
With the 1.18 release,
Go now supports

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.920
providing parameterized
types in a couple

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.310
of different circumstances,
functions, structs,

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.050
and/or methods.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.390
Here, we can see that
this function has

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.970
one parameterized type
T that is constrained

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.400
by the constraints ordered.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.680
Constraining by
this value not only

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.920
allows us to pass in different
types like floats and strings

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.870
into this function,
it also allows

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.330
us to use the operators
that work on those types,

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:29.240
like the less than
operator in this example.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.510
Parameterized types also
work on structs and methods.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.870
The struct here specifies
one parameterized type T

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.470
that is constrained to the
types defined by the any,

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:44.420
which is equivalent to the empty
interface, meaning, all types.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.670
Notice that the
corresponding method we

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:48.770
don't need to provide
a constraint for T

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:52.760
again as it's already
defined by the struct.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.790
Let's break down what
an interface in Go

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:58.760
is prior to 1.18.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.330
Historically, interfaces
define method sets.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.090
Any type that implements
all of the methods defined

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:09.380
by an interface implicitly
implements the interface.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.420
Let's present that
same information

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:17.060
in another way, in relation to
the types and not method sets.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.970
Let's say that an
interface defines

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.490
a set of types, which
are the types that

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:26.090
implement the methods
defined by the interface.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.970
Any type that is a member
of the interfaces type set

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:31.970
implements the interface.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.340
In the end, both of
these views of interfaces

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.480
lead to the same outcome.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.590
But this new way of
describing an interface has

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:42.530
an advantage that we
can explicitly add types

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.990
to the set of
interface describes,

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:47.150
allowing us to
control the typeset

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:49.250
in new and different ways.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:52.190
With Go 1.18, the
syntax for interfaces

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.230
has been expanded
to optionally allow

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.330
specifying a set of types.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:58.970
If this type set is
provided, the interface

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:03.670
can only be implemented by types
that specified in the type set.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.590
As we saw in the
previous example,

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.370
parameterized values must also
define constraints that scope

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.800
how the type may be used.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.080
In Go, we already have
a concept that can

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.300
scope how a type can be used.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.540
Interfaces, type
constraints are interfaces

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.670
that may contain some
extra information.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.870
All interfaces can, but
not necessarily should,

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.570
be used as type constraints.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.030
The inverse is not
true though, not

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.550
all things that can
be used as constraints

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.560
can be used as interfaces.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.410
Here's an example of how
constraints package define

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.180
the sign interface.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.280
The sign interface
defines a set of all the

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.140
built in signed types.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:45.880
The pipe symbol
between the types

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.870
is used to express that we are
taking the union of the type

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:50.650
set.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.420
Also note that we
don't need to provide

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.850
methods for this interface.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.540
Since operators like plus,
minus, and less than all

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.850
work for the types
defined by this interface,

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.970
any parameterized type that is
constrained by this interface

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:09.290
may also use those operators.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.480
We don't always want to
restrict an interface

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:12.740
to a particular type.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.660
Sometimes, we want
the definition

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.480
to be a little more fuzzy.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.820
This is where the new
tilde token comes in.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:21.950
When put in front of a
type, it means the set

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:25.820
of all user defined types whose
underlying type is that type.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:29.120
This means that the type
myInt64 in the example

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:33.100
also satisfies the
signed interface.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.030
Now that we have learned the
concepts behind Go's generics,

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.550
let's take a quick look
at how they compare

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.670
to using generics in Java.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.520
One of the biggest differences
is that Go does not

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:46.140
have type erasure.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.600
This means that at
runtime, Go retains

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.600
all of the type information
associated with the calling

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.650
function or method.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:56.010
Here we can see, by using
the reflect package,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.380
that the type information
of the parameterized type

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.690
is preserved at runtime.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.120
Another difference is
that Go also does not

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.150
have the idea of wildcards.

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:09.720
Since Go is not a traditional
object-oriented language,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.090
as it does not have
type hierarchies,

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:15.000
there is no need for wildcards.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.460
Like the rest of the
language, Go's generics

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:21.882
are designed with
simplicity in mind.

00:07:21.882 --> 00:07:24.340
This means some of the more
complicated features associated

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:28.330
with generics in other
languages are not present in Go.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:31.300
These include, no
specialization that

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.480
allows writing multiple versions
of a generic function designed

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.900
to work with specific
types, no metaprogramming,

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.030
allowing code to be
written at compile time

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:43.090
to generate code to be
executed at runtime,

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:47.170
no contravariance or covariance,
for the same reason Go doesn't

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:51.130
support wildcards, no
operator overloading

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:55.850
for things like accessing a
map with the bracket syntax.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:59.320
Now that we have learned what
Go generics are and are not,

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.050
let's take a look and see
where using generics in Go

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:03.100
make sense.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.470
One example of a
good use case is

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:07.060
when you are writing
functions that

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:10.120
work with container types
that can be used to process

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.100
elements of any types.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.350
For example, here we
have a function that

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.540
checks if two slices are equal.

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.670
To do this operation,
we only need

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:21.010
to be able to compare the
elements of the slice.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:24.340
So we use the built-in
constraint comparable.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:26.540
That allows us to do just that.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.630
Another case you might
consider using generics

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:32.350
is when different types need
to implement some common logic

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:34.690
and the implementation
looks the same

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:38.110
for all of the different
implementing types.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:40.210
For example, let's
consider the case

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.340
where we need to sort a slice.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:44.680
The standard library
defines a sort interface

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:49.360
that requires three methods,
len, swap, and less.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.130
This example shows
the generic struck

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.860
that implements the methods
for all splice types.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.520
This can be done because
the logic for all types

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.530
is exactly the same.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:02.290
Here, we can see how the
previously-defined slice func

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:06.220
type is used to sort any slice
that can provide a comparison

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:08.020
function.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.880
Here, type parameters
can be used

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:14.380
to make it easy for any type to
implement the sort interface.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:16.840
Type parameters worked well
here because the implementation

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:20.070
looks the same for all types.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:23.190
We have seen some examples
of where to use generics.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:27.610
Now let's take a look and see
where to not use generics.

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:29.800
As discussed earlier,
basic interfaces

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:32.290
already provide a
generic programming in Go

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:34.090
prior to 1.18.

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:37.030
If you were only calling
methods to find on an interface,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.880
it is better to not
use type parameters.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.150
For example, this function
defines a parameterized type T

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:45.640
that is constrained
by io.Reader.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:49.690
io.Reader does not define a
typeset, just to read method.

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:53.140
Adding a type parameterization
here just adds complexity

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.930
and provides no extra value.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:58.390
A function instantiated
with a type argument

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:01.120
will most likely not
be any faster than code

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.680
that uses interface methods.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:05.110
Avoid using generics
in cases like these.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.030
Removing the previous
parameterized as type

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:10.240
helps make this function
signature more readable.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:12.970
Another place we
should not use generics

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:16.090
is if the implementation
is different for each type.

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:18.730
In this case, just
use different methods,

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:22.110
and don't try to force
generics into your code.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.410
For example, the
implementation from reading

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:26.780
from a file on disk
and a network socket

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:28.680
are quite different.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:32.840
So different methods should
be defined for each operation.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:35.480
Also, there are times
when using reflection

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.730
might make more sense
than using generics.

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:39.500
An example in the
standard library

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:41.540
is the encoding JSON package.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.970
If we required the
use of an interface,

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:46.430
that would require
every type passed in

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:48.560
to define a Marshal JSON method.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:51.920
This would be quite
cumbersome, and different types

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.560
are encoded differently.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.560
Generics doesn't make
sense in this case.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.810
Now let's take everything
we've learned so far

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:01.310
and see how we can
apply what we've

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.860
learned about generics
to simplify a simple web

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:06.890
app written for Go 1.16.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:09.860
Let's take a look at the web app
I have deployed to Cloud Run.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:13.220
It's currently deployed with
the Go 1.16 runtime, which

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.110
just stopped receiving
security updates

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:17.070
with the release of 1.18.

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.460
So it's time to update
to the latest runtime.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:22.520
The first step is to change the
version declared in the go.mod

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:27.210
to 1.18.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:29.490
Next, let's take a
look at our main.go

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.650
and see what's going
on in our web app.

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.280
The first thing I
notice right away

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.650
is that we seem to be
generating some generic types

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:42.210
with a tool called genny.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:46.090
Let's take a look at that code.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:48.760
Here, it looks like we're
defining a generic number

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:50.440
type that will
later be generated

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.350
into concrete functions
for each type.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:56.950
The function here is taking the
Max of the elements in a slice.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:12:00.920
Let's go take a look
at the generated code.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:02.750
It appears that
the two functions

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:04.520
are generated for the
two different types

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:05.780
that we are working with.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:09.230
This is a great example of a
place we can refactor our code

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:10.460
to use generics.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:12.900
So let's do it.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.820
I'm going to start
by copy and pasting

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:16.950
one of the implementations
into our main.go.

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:26.610
This function wants to be
generic over the types int

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:28.110
and float 64.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:29.790
So let's first
define a constraint

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.020
representing those types.

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:42.340
I'm going to make use of the
golang/x/exp library, which

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.320
provides some
common constraints.

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:58.300
I run go mod tidy here to
pull in the new dependency.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.840
Now let's substitute
out our int type,

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:14.530
the generic type T bound
by our number constraint.

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:29.250
Now we rename the function
since its general purpose.

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:35.310
Next, let's find
the spots that call

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.525
the old generated
functions and replace them

00:13:37.525 --> 00:13:38.775
with our new generic function.

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:56.610
You will notice that although
I could provide a type, when

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.620
calling the function,
I don't need

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:02.310
to because the compiler is smart
enough to infer the type for us

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:03.600
in this instance.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.270
Now that we have replaced
the old call sites,

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:09.010
we get to do my favorite
part, delete some code.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:11.265
Let's clean up the code that
is no longer being used.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:20.070
It also looks like we no longer
need the dependency on genny.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:23.310
So let's run go mod tidy to
simplify our dependency tree.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:37.940
Sorry, I got a little
excited and jumped straight

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.780
into refactoring some code.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.510
Let's take a step
back and see what else

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:43.200
is going on in main.go.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:46.180
Let's start with
the main function.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:47.800
It looks like what's
happening here

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:50.920
is we are registering
to HTTP handlers, one

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:53.440
that processes ints
and one that processes

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.840
floats, then we are just
starting up a server.

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:01.460
Scrolling down a
little more, it looks

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:04.310
like we have a response type
that works with float 64s.

00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:10.880
Next, we have our
process ints handler.

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:14.150
It looks like this decode
some JSON into a slice events,

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.490
then takes the summoned
Max of that slice,

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:19.190
and finally, marshals
the data back to JSON

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:23.140
and writes the response
to the calling client.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:24.970
When creating the
response type, it

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:29.050
appears we are casting the int
and some Max to a float 64.

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:34.150
Maybe instead of this, we can
just make some response generic

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:35.060
as well.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.190
Let's do that.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:38.260
I again, will make use
of the number constraint

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:39.475
we defined earlier.

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:52.805
And now we just need to
refactor where the type is used.

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:10.960
When instantiating
types, we need

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:15.820
to provide the parameterized
type we are generic over.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:18.970
It looks like the sum variable
returned from Sum function

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:20.470
is not the right type.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.570
Let's take a closer
look at the Sum function

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:27.470
to see if we can do some
more refactoring there.

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:29.693
It looks like a lot
is going on here.

00:16:29.693 --> 00:16:31.610
The first thing I noticed
is that the function

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:35.180
takes an empty interface, which
is a good first signal that we

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.310
may be able to refactor it.

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:41.120
Next, I notice that we cast the
input parameter to a slice then

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.910
type switch on the
element type, then

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:47.300
it appears we take the sum of
either an int or a float 64.

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:50.780
This code can definitely
benefit from a refactor.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:53.990
We will again use the number
constraint we defined earlier

00:16:53.990 --> 00:17:03.050
and make the function
generic over T.

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:05.780
I know that this function
should expect a slice of T.

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:08.450
So let's change the input
parameter to match that?

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:16.089
Let's also change
the return value to T

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:18.384
so that the function
operates only on one type.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.629
Now we just clean up the rest.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:57.060
That looks much better.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.080
Scrolling back up
to our int handler,

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:05.320
it looks like the issue we
saw earlier is now resolved.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:07.330
No more red squiggles.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.460
I think we are good with
this handler for now.

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:11.080
Let's move on to
the float handler.

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:17.120
This appears to be almost
identical to our ints handler

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:20.210
above, but it
operates on float 64s.

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:23.270
First we decode some JSON,
we process the input,

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:26.750
and then marshal the data back
to JSON and write the result.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:28.550
Besides the business
logic and types

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:32.280
these two handlers use,
these seem very similar.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:35.330
Let's see if we can do some
more generic refactoring here

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:37.730
and make a general HTTP handler.

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.130
Let's start by copying one
of the existing handlers.

00:18:45.770 --> 00:18:48.350
We will start refactoring
by making this function

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:50.660
return in http handler func.

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:52.250
This will let us
turn the function

00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:55.580
into a middleware we can wrap
our int and float handler with.

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:20.190
Now let's figure out
how we can abstract away

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:21.720
our business logic.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.690
Let's have this middleware be
generic over a function that

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:40.850
takes the input I and
returns the output O. Here,

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.250
I'm making use of the
any constraint, that

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:46.110
is a new keyword, and
as mentioned earlier,

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:48.080
is just an alias for
the empty interface.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:51.180
Meaning we can pass
any type to it.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.790
Next, let's refactor out
the business logic out

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:57.000
of the generic handler and
have just those bits in the int

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.050
and float handlers.

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:18.320
In our original handlers, we can
delete a lot of the common code

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.780
and adjust the input and
output types accordingly.

00:20:57.740 --> 00:20:59.990
We can actually take
things one step further

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:02.720
and reduce this down
to one generic process

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:04.925
handler that is constrained
by the number again.

00:21:04.925 --> 00:21:06.425
Let's see what that
would look like.

00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:31.210
Finally, we just need to wrap
our tight parameterized generic

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:33.550
handler in the
generic middleware.

00:21:56.470 --> 00:21:58.510
We just did quite a
bit of refactoring

00:21:58.510 --> 00:21:59.870
in a short amount of time.

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:01.693
Let's try starting
up the server locally

00:22:01.693 --> 00:22:03.610
and testing it with the
little client I wrote.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:24.470
Hey, we did it.

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:25.580
It works.

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:28.820
I have this workload deployed
to Cloud Run on Google Cloud.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:32.900
So our last step is to ship
it, gcloud, run deploy.

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:35.810
And in a few moments, we
would have our new generic app

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:38.210
up and running on the Cloud.

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:40.970
In this small example, we
were able to reduce our code

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:45.320
base by over 40% and get
rid of a dependency, all

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:48.170
by adopting generics.

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:50.300
I hope that this helped
reinforce some of the ways

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:53.750
that adopting generics can
simplify your code base.

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:56.515
Back to you, Cameron.

